 ### [Exact Visibility Ordering for Cell Projection Volume Rendering (SXMPVO)](944-exact-visibility-ordering-for-cell-projection-volume-renderi.md)
-SXMPVO extends MPVO to non-convex meshes by replacing expensive object-precision ray shooting with efficient scan conversion of exterior faces into an A-Buffer to determine occlusion relations (Pseudoneighbor or PN relations) between cells. It guarantees image-precision visibility ordering, matching the rendering resolution, and avoids BSP tree construction.
+SXMPVO is an algorithm that computes image-precision visibility ordering for volume rendering of unstructured meshes by scan-converting exterior faces into an A-Buffer to determine occlusion relations (Pseudoneighbor or PN relations) between cells, rather than computing object-precision ray shooting queries. It guarantees correct visibility order for a particular image resolution by using image-precision scan conversion rather than strict object-space visibility ordering. It sets itself apart from its parent [Topological Sort for Cell Projection](999-topological-sort-for-cell-projection.md) and sibling [XMPVO](1363-xmpvo.md) by replacing expensive object-precision ray shooting with efficient scan conversion, achieving "image-space correctness" where the rendering resolution matches the ordering resolution. Unlike [BSP-XMPVO](1160-bsp-xmpvo.md), it avoids BSP tree construction and exact arithmetic, making it simpler and faster for general acyclic meshes.
 
 ### [A-buffer visibility sort for non-convex grids](1057-a-buffer-visibility-sort-for-non-convex-grids.md)
-This method extends MPVO to handle non-convex grids by resolving visibility gaps between boundary cells using an image-space A-Buffer. It solves the problem where viewing rays exit the grid through an external face, traverse empty space, and re-enter the grid, creating occlusion relationships not captured by shared-face topology alone.
+This method extends the [Topological Sort for Cell Projection](999-topological-sort-for-cell-projection.md) to handle non-convex grids by resolving visibility gaps between boundary cells using an image-space A-buffer. It solves the problem where viewing rays exit the grid through an external face, traverse empty space, and re-enter the grid, creating occlusion relationships that are not captured by shared-face topology alone. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Exact Visibility Ordering for Cell Projection Volume Rendering (SXMPVO)](944-exact-visibility-ordering-for-cell-projection-volume-renderi.md) by avoiding expensive pairwise geometric occlusion tests or BSP-tree construction, instead relying on rasterization to implicitly determine segment ordering.
 
 ### [Power Distance Sort](1060-power-distance-sort.md)
-Power Distance Sort orders primitives back-to-front by comparing the power of the viewpoint with respect to the circumcircles/circumspheres of the primitives. It leverages Delaunay properties to guarantee correct ordering for Delaunay meshes but is not guaranteed for non-Delaunay meshes, unlike MPVO which relies on topology.
+Power Distance Sort is a visibility sorting method that orders primitives (triangles or tetrahedra) back-to-front relative to a camera origin by comparing the power of the viewpoint with respect to the circumcircles or circumspheres of the primitives. It leverages geometric properties of Delaunay triangulations/tetrahedralizations to guarantee correct visibility ordering, whereas it is not guaranteed to produce accurate ordering for non-Delaunay meshes. Unlike topological sort, it avoids storing and accessing an adjacency structure.
 
 ### [Per-Pixel Sorting (General Variant)](1061-per-pixel-sorting-general-variant.md)
-This image-space method sorts all cell faces projecting onto a specific pixel at render time. It solves visibility cycles by sorting locally per pixel, whereas MPVO requires a global topological sort and fails on cycles.
+Per-Pixel Sorting (General Variant) is an image-space visibility ordering method that sorts all cell faces projecting onto a specific pixel at render time, just prior to compositing. It solves the problem of determining the correct back-to-front rendering order for transparent volumes in scenes where a global topological sort is impossible due to visibility cycles. Unlike its sibling [Exact Visibility Ordering for Cell Projection Volume Rendering (SXMPVO)](944-exact-visibility-ordering-for-cell-projection-volume-renderi.md), which sorts only exterior faces and exploits spatial coherence, this variant sorts all faces (including interior ones) independently or incrementally per pixel. It differs from [Mammen's Technique](1279-mammen-s-technique.md) in that it sorts all cell faces per pixel, whereas Mammen's technique specifically sorts transparent fragments in front of the front-most opaque one.
 
 ### [Newell-based Visibility Sorting](1123-newell-based-visibility-sorting.md)
-Newell-based Visibility Sorting extends the Newell, Newell, and Sancha polygon sorting method to polyhedra. It sorts cells based on geometric depth and area properties rather than strict topological adjacency, providing an alternative when topological relations are insufficient.
+Newell-based Visibility Sorting is an algorithm to sort cells into a back-to-front ordered list for correct hardware compositing. By definition, if cell A partially occludes cell B from a particular viewpoint V, cell B must come before cell A in the list. It extends the Newell, Newell, and Sancha polygon sorting method to the case of polyhedra.
 
 ### [XMPVO](1363-xmpvo.md)
-XMPVO improves MPVO's runtime for non-convex meshes by using ray shooting queries to discover exterior face relations. It performs these checks in object-space precision, unlike SXMPVO which uses image-space.
+XMPVO is an algorithm that improves the runtime of the Meshed Polyhedra Visibility Ordering (MPVO) method by using ray shooting queries to discover exterior face relations for nonconvex meshes. It determines the correct back-to-front rendering order for convex volumes filled with convex polyhedral cells when the standard topological sort is insufficient due to occlusions between exterior faces. It sets itself apart from its parent [Topological Sort for Cell Projection](999-topological-sort-for-cell-projection.md) by augmenting the graph with geometric visibility checks, and from its sibling [Exact Visibility Ordering for Cell Projection Volume Rendering (SXMPVO)](944-exact-visibility-ordering-for-cell-projection-volume-renderi.md) by performing these checks in object-space precision rather than image-space.
 
 ### [MPVONC](1377-mpvonc.md)
-MPVONC is a heuristic sorting algorithm for non-convex polyhedral cells. It extends MPVO by handling exterior cells lacking transitive "behind" relationships via shared faces. It prioritizes speed over correctness, making it prone to visual artifacts in specific occlusion scenarios.
+MPVONC is a heuristic sorting algorithm used in cell projection volume rendering to order non-convex polyhedral cells back-to-front. It extends the exact MPVO algorithm by handling exterior cells that lack a transitive "behind" relationship via shared faces, which MPVO cannot resolve alone. Unlike exact methods such as SXMPVO or A-buffer sorts, MPVONC prioritizes speed over correctness, making it "accurate enough" for many applications but prone to visual artifacts in specific occlusion scenarios.
 
 ### [Convex Grid Topological Visibility Sorting](2370-convex-grid-topological-visibility-sorting.md)
-This is an $O(n)$ algorithm for convex grids of convex cells. It is essentially the core MPVO algorithm, relying exclusively on local face-sharing topology rather than global geometric tests or distance metrics.
+Convex Grid Topological Visibility Sorting is an $O(n)$ algorithm that orders cells in a convex grid of convex polyhedral cells back-to-front for semi-transparent volume rendering. It constructs a directed adjacency graph where edges represent pairwise occlusion relations derived from the viewpoint position, then performs a topological sort to produce a valid compositing order. It sets itself apart from [Exact Visibility Ordering for Cell Projection Volume Rendering (SXMPVO)](944-exact-visibility-ordering-for-cell-projection-volume-renderi.md) and [Power Distance Sort](1060-power-distance-sort.md) by relying exclusively on local face-sharing topology rather than global geometric tests or distance metrics, provided the grid volume is convex.
 
